<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ICT3113 Assignment 1 — mixed_load.jmx
  Owner: Part 1 — Yeo Kai Yuan (test harness).  Feeds slides 8 and 9.

  The mixed-workload plan. Two independent open-loop arrival streams hit the same
  service at the same time:
      * the complaint intake            POST /tickets           at rate_per_min
      * the agents' desk searching      GET  /search            at search_rate_per_min

  This is the plan that answers a response-time requirement of the form "GET
  /search must stay under X ms at the p95 while tickets are being classified at
  Y per minute". The two streams matter together and not separately: POST
  /tickets blocks on a CPU-only model call, and GET /search does a full-table
  LIKE scan in the same single-worker process, so the interesting question is
  what each does to the other.

  Hand-written rather than GUI-generated so that it stays reviewable in a diff.
  See jmeter/README.md for the property list.
-->
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="ICT3113 A1 — mixed load: POST /tickets + GET /search" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">WHY BOTH THREAD GROUPS ARE OPEN-LOOP, AND WHY THAT MATTERS EVEN MORE HERE.

Both streams use an Open Model Thread Group, which is an open-loop (arrival-rate)
model: JMeter decides in advance when each request arrives and starts a thread
for it, whether or not earlier requests have been answered.

A closed-loop thread group would self-throttle. Its offered rate is
threads / response_time, so as soon as the service slows the generator slows with
it, no queue builds up, and the interference between the two streams — the whole
point of this plan — disappears from the measurement. On a mixed plan it is worse
than on a single-endpoint plan: if POSTs slow down under load, a closed-loop
search stream would also slow down, so we would never see what a search costs an
agent while the classifier is saturated.

The assignment brief is explicit: "Traffic must be generated open-loop, at
controlled arrival rates: use the Open Model Thread Group or the Precise
Throughput Timer... Results from closed-loop tests will not be accepted as
evidence against throughput or latency requirements."

TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups is FALSE and must stay false. Serialising the
groups would run the searches after the tickets instead of during them, and this
plan would then measure two separate single-endpoint tests under a misleading name.

BASELINE NOTE. Nothing here makes the service look fast: no caching, no think
time, no reuse across samples, no client-side retries.
# A2 candidate: none in this file — the load generator is not what we optimise.</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">true</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <!-- ===================================================================
           STREAM 1 — the complaint intake. Identical in every respect to
           jmeter/load_post_tickets.jmx, so that a mixed run and a pure-POST run
           at the same rate are directly comparable.
           =================================================================== -->
      <org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup guiclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.gui.OpenModelThreadGroupGui" testclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup" testname="Ticket intake" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Schedule: rate(${__P(rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec)

Byte-for-byte the schedule used by jmeter/load_post_tickets.jmx. Keep it that way:
the value of this plan is that the POST numbers can be compared against the
pure-POST plan at the same rate_per_min, and the only difference between the two
runs is the presence of the search stream.

random_seed = ${__P(random_seed,0)}, i.e. a fresh arrival pattern per run.
If a fixed pattern is wanted for reproducibility, pass -Jrandom_seed AND
-Jsearch_random_seed with DIFFERENT non-zero values: JMeter's documentation warns
that two thread groups sharing one non-zero seed can fire their samples at the
same instants, which would manufacture a collision that no real workload has.</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.schedule">rate(${__P(rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec)</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.random_seed">${__P(random_seed,0)}</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroupController"/>
      </org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="Ticket narratives (CSV Data Set Config)" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Same configuration as jmeter/load_post_tickets.jmx: columns
row_number,narrative,raw_label; quoted data because narratives contain commas and
newlines; header line skipped and re-skipped on recycle; recycled at EOF so the
offered rate is sustained for the whole duration; one shared file handle.

Defaults to the synthetic dev tickets. No team row may reach a model before the
golden set and prediction record are committed and tagged; run_load_test.sh
passes -Jinput_csv and only passes data/team_rows.csv after the freeze gate passes.

Placed inside this thread group rather than at test-plan level so that the search
stream does not also consume rows from it.</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="filename">${__P(input_csv,../data/dev/synthetic_tickets.csv)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="variableNames">row_number,narrative,raw_label</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="quotedData">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        </CSVDataSet>
        <hashTree/>
        <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager — content type and reconciliation headers" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">X-Request-ID is the join key between this .jtl and logs/service/*.jsonl;
analysis/reconcile.py joins on it. X-Source-Row carries the course CSV row number
so a log line can be traced to the exact ticket text. Content-Type must be
application/json for FastAPI to parse the body.</stringProp>
          <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
            <elementProp name="Content-Type" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Request-ID" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Request-ID</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${request_id}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Source-Row" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Source-Row</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${source_row}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </HeaderManager>
        <hashTree/>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="POST /tickets" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">The sampler name is the .jtl label column and analysis/ groups by it. It is
deliberately the same string as in jmeter/load_post_tickets.jmx so the two plans'
POST results line up. Do not rename it.

host has no usable default: the brief requires the load generator on a separate
machine from the service, so the default below is an invalid hostname that fails
loudly rather than quietly measuring a co-hosted run. Pass -Jhost.

response_timeout_ms defaults to 180000, longer than the service's
OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S of 120 s, so a slow model call is recorded as the service's
latency and not as a load-generator timeout.</stringProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="Request body" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">false</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">{"narrative": ${narrative_json}}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${__P(host,SET-JHOST-TO-THE-SERVICE-HOST)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${__P(port,8000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.contentEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/tickets</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.follow_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.auto_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.DO_MULTIPART_POST">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">${__P(connect_timeout_ms,10000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">${__P(response_timeout_ms,180000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.embedded_url_re"></stringProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <UserParameters guiclass="UserParametersGui" testclass="UserParameters" testname="Per-iteration ids (request_id, source_row)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">request_id = ${__UUID()} ; source_row = ${row_number}.

per_iteration is FALSE on purpose. With it true these values would be set from an
iterationStart listener, and JMeter notifies iteration listeners in reverse
registration order, so this element could fire before the CSV Data Set Config had
advanced to the current row — every source_row would then name the previous
ticket and every reconciled row would be off by one, invisibly. With it false the
values are set from process(), i.e. immediately before the sampler, once the CSV
row for this iteration is already bound.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.names">
              <stringProp name="37109963">request_id</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="-1111110730">source_row</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.thread_values">
              <collectionProp name="-1">
                <stringProp name="118040362">${__UUID()}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="2006504376">${row_number}</stringProp>
              </collectionProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <boolProp name="UserParameters.per_iteration">false</boolProp>
          </UserParameters>
          <hashTree/>
          <JSR223PreProcessor guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="JSR223PreProcessor" testname="JSON-escape the narrative (escaping, not logic)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">THIS IS ESCAPING, NOT LOGIC. JMeter has no JSON-escape function and our
narratives contain double quotes, newlines and non-ASCII punctuation, so the body
cannot be built by string concatenation. JsonOutput.toJson() returns the value
already wrapped in double quotes, which is why the body template is
{"narrative": ${narrative_json}} with no quotes of its own.

cacheKey makes Groovy compile the script once per run instead of once per sample,
so the generator does not burn CPU inside a measured interval.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="cacheKey">ict3113-narrative-json-escape</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="filename"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="parameters"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="scriptLanguage">groovy</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="script">// Escaping only. See this element's comment.
// Names no Java type on purpose: JMeter 5.6.3's Groovy cannot compile one on a
// newer JDK and the failure is silent. See jmeter/README.md, "Groovy and the JDK
// version". JsonOutput is a Groovy class, which is safe.
vars.put('narrative_json', groovy.json.JsonOutput.toJson(vars.get('narrative')))</stringProp>
          </JSR223PreProcessor>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="HTTP 200" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Response code equals 200, and nothing else. There is deliberately no assertion
on the returned category: that would be measuring classification accuracy
client-side inside a timed load test. Accuracy is measured only by
scripts/run_accuracy.py against the frozen golden set. A 200 carrying
category = "UNPARSEABLE" is correctly counted as a success here — the request was
served; the reply being unusable is an accuracy question, not a performance one.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="49586">200</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">8</intProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Expected HTTP 200 from POST /tickets. A 502 is a model error or timeout, a 422 is a malformed request body — check logs/service/*.jsonl for this request_id.</stringProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
      </hashTree>
      <!-- ===================================================================
           STREAM 2 — the agents' desk. Runs concurrently with stream 1.
           =================================================================== -->
      <org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup guiclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.gui.OpenModelThreadGroupGui" testclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup" testname="Agent searches" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Schedule: rate(${__P(search_rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec)

The duration is the SAME property as the intake stream's, so the two streams
start together and stop together and every search happens while tickets are being
classified. Do not give the search stream its own duration: a search stream that
outlives the intake would contribute uncontended samples to the same percentile.

TODO(Part 4 — Teammate C): set the search rate from the workload model. The
default of 60/min is deliberately the same number as rate_per_min's default so
that it is visibly a placeholder and not a measured agent-to-ticket ratio. The
brief requires the workload model to estimate "the rate of agent-side searches"
with a cited source; put the figure in ../workload/requirements.md and pass
--search-rate to scripts/run_load_test.sh. Nothing in this repository invents it.

random_seed = ${__P(search_random_seed,0)} — a separate property from the intake
stream's on purpose. If reproducible arrival patterns are wanted, the two streams
must be given DIFFERENT non-zero seeds; JMeter's documentation warns that two
thread groups sharing one non-zero seed can fire at the same instants.</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.schedule">rate(${__P(search_rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec)</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.random_seed">${__P(search_random_seed,0)}</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroupController"/>
      </org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="Search terms (CSV Data Set Config)" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">One term per line, read from ../data/search_terms.txt. Not really a CSV at all:
the whole line is the term.

quotedData is FALSE and the delimiter is a TAB, which together are what make the
whole line the term. This is not cosmetic and was found the hard way: with quoted
data enabled and a comma delimiter JMeter parses every line as CSV, and a prose
comment line containing a double quote raises "Cannot have quote-char in plain
field". That exception aborts the iteration, so the sample is never sent — which
silently drops an arrival and destroys the one property an open-loop test must
have. With quoting off there is no parse step to fail, and a term may contain
commas and quotes freely.

ignoreFirstLine is TRUE, which skips exactly one line and re-skips it on every
recycle. The terms file is hand-maintained and headed by a '#' comment block, and
JMeter's CSV Data Set Config has no notion of a comment, so it would otherwise
hand those lines to the sampler as though an agent had typed them. One line is all
JMeter can skip; the JSR223 pre-processor on the sampler below deals with the rest
of the block and with any '#' line a teammate adds later. See jmeter/README.md,
section "Why the search plan filters the terms file".

recycle=true, stopThread=false: a 300 s run at any useful rate needs more searches
than the file has terms, and an open-loop stream must keep offering its rate.

Placed inside this thread group so the intake stream does not consume terms.</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="filename">${__P(search_terms_file,../data/search_terms.txt)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="variableNames">search_term</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="delimiter">\t</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="quotedData">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        </CSVDataSet>
        <hashTree/>
        <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager — reconciliation header" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">X-Request-ID only. A search is not a ticket, so there is no course CSV row to
report and X-Source-Row is deliberately NOT sent: the log line contract says
source_row is null for /search and /stats lines, and sending an empty header
would put an empty string there instead of a null.

analysis/reconcile.py joins on request_id, which every sample carries, so search
samples reconcile with their log lines exactly as POST samples do.

No Content-Type: this is a GET with no body.</stringProp>
          <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
            <elementProp name="X-Request-ID" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Request-ID</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${request_id}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </HeaderManager>
        <hashTree/>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="GET /search" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">GET /search?q=&lt;term&gt;&amp;limit=50

The sampler name is the .jtl label column; analysis/ groups by it. Do not rename.

limit is the literal 50 fixed by the build contract. It is the parameter that
decides how much JSON the service has to serialise for a query that matches many
tickets, so changing it changes what the response-time requirement means. If a
different page size is ever tested, add a second sampler rather than editing this
one, so old and new results stay distinguishable in the .jtl by label.

q is sent as a request parameter with always_encode=true, so multi-word terms are
URL-encoded by JMeter and the terms file needs no escaping.

Note what is NOT asserted: nothing about the number of results. A term that
matches no stored ticket still costs the service a full-table LIKE scan, which is
the work this plan is here to measure.
-- A2 candidate (service side, recorded here because this is the plan that
-- exposes it): SQLite FTS5 or an index instead of a full-table LIKE scan.</stringProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="Query parameters" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="q" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">true</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.name">q</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">${search_q}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.use_equals">true</boolProp>
              </elementProp>
              <elementProp name="limit" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">true</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.name">limit</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">50</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.use_equals">true</boolProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${__P(host,SET-JHOST-TO-THE-SERVICE-HOST)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${__P(port,8000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.contentEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/search</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">GET</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.follow_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.auto_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.DO_MULTIPART_POST">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">${__P(connect_timeout_ms,10000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">${__P(response_timeout_ms,180000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.embedded_url_re"></stringProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <UserParameters guiclass="UserParametersGui" testclass="UserParameters" testname="Per-iteration ids (request_id, source_row)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">request_id = ${__UUID()} ; source_row = "" (empty).

source_row is declared and set to empty on purpose rather than left undefined, so
that the source_row column of the .jtl is deterministically empty for every
search row and a reader can see from this element why. A search has no course CSV
row behind it. The Header Manager above does not send X-Source-Row at all, so the
service records source_row as null for these requests, as the log contract requires.

per_iteration is FALSE for the same reason as in the intake stream: process() runs
immediately before the sampler, after the CSV Data Set Config has bound this
iteration's term, whereas an iterationStart listener could fire before it.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.names">
              <stringProp name="37109963">request_id</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="-1111110730">source_row</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.thread_values">
              <collectionProp name="-1">
                <stringProp name="118040362">${__UUID()}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="0"></stringProp>
              </collectionProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <boolProp name="UserParameters.per_iteration">false</boolProp>
          </UserParameters>
          <hashTree/>
          <JSR223PreProcessor guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="JSR223PreProcessor" testname="Skip comment and blank lines from the terms file (line filtering, not logic)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">THIS IS LINE FILTERING, NOT TEST LOGIC. It decides nothing about the workload; it
only stops a comment line in a hand-maintained file from being sent as a query.

Why it exists: ../data/search_terms.txt is edited by hand and its format allows
'#' comment lines and blank lines — the file is headed by a TODO block for Part 4.
JMeter's CSV Data Set Config has no concept of a comment, so it hands those lines
to this sampler as though an agent had typed them.

Why it substitutes instead of skipping the sample: skipping would drop an arrival,
and an open-loop test's one non-negotiable property is that the offered rate is
the rate we configured. So a comment or blank line is replaced by one of the
file's OWN usable terms. That keeps the offered rate exact and invents no term
that is not already in the file.

The substitution pool is the set of usable terms this run has already seen, kept
in a JMeter property as one newline-separated string. It is deliberately NOT built
by re-reading the terms file from this script: see jmeter/README.md, section
"Groovy and the JDK version" — the Groovy that ships with JMeter 5.6.3 cannot
compile a script that names a Java type such as File on a JDK newer than it
expects, and its failure kills the sampler SILENTLY, with no error in jmeter.log
and no row in the .jtl. This script therefore names no Java type at all and uses
only JMeter's own bindings. Keep it that way.

The consequence, stated plainly: at the very start of a run the pool is empty, so
the leading '#' lines of the terms file that ignoreFirstLine did not skip are sent
as queries. With the file as committed that is the first few searches of a run —
about 2% of a 300 s run at 60/min, all of them at the beginning. They are real
requests that cost the service a real full-table scan and match nothing, so they
are not excluded from the results; they are simply not realistic queries. If that
matters for a reported run, move the comment block to the END of the terms file:
by the time JMeter reaches it the pool holds every term and no query is ever a
comment.

A blank line in the terms file arrives as JMeter's &lt;EOF&gt; marker rather than as an
empty string, because splitting an empty line yields no fields at all and
CSVDataSet then sets every variable to csvdataset.eofstring. That is treated here
exactly like a comment.

cacheKey makes Groovy compile this once per run rather than once per sample.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="cacheKey">ict3113-search-term-line-filter</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="filename"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="parameters"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="scriptLanguage">groovy</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="script">// Line filtering only. See this element's comment for why it exists.
// NO Java type names anywhere in this script, on purpose: JMeter 5.6.3's Groovy
// cannot compile them on a newer JDK and the failure is silent. Bindings only.
def KEY = 'ict3113.search_terms_seen'

def term = vars.get('search_term')
if (term == null) { term = '' }
term = term.trim()

// A blank line in the terms file arrives as the &lt;EOF&gt; marker, not as '';
// treat it like a comment line.
def usable = !term.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; !term.startsWith('#') &amp;&amp; term != '&lt;EOF&gt;'

def stored = props.get(KEY)
if (stored == null) { stored = '' }

if (usable) {
    // Remember it, so a later comment line has a real term to be replaced by.
    if (!stored.readLines().contains(term)) {
        props.put(KEY, stored.isEmpty() ? term : stored + '\n' + term)
    }
} else {
    def pool = stored.readLines()
    if (pool.size() &gt; 0) {
        // ctx.getThreadNum() increments with every arrival in an Open Model
        // Thread Group, so successive substitutions rotate through the pool
        // instead of all landing on the same term.
        term = pool[ctx.getThreadNum() % pool.size()]
    }
}
vars.put('search_q', term)</stringProp>
          </JSR223PreProcessor>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="HTTP 200" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Response code equals 200, and nothing else. In particular there is no assertion
on the number of results returned: a query that matches nothing is a perfectly
valid search and still costs the service a full-table scan. Asserting on the
result set would turn a data question into a spurious error rate.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="49586">200</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">8</intProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Expected HTTP 200 from GET /search. Check logs/service/*.jsonl for this request_id.</stringProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
      </hashTree>
      <ResultCollector guiclass="SimpleDataWriter" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Simple Data Writer (only when -Jjtl_out is set)" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">scripts/run_load_test.sh writes results with JMeter's -l flag, whose own result
collector is configured from the jmeter.save.saveservice.* settings in
jmeter/user.properties. That file, not this element, is the authoritative save
configuration and is what appends the request_id and source_row columns.

This element only writes when -Jjtl_out=/absolute/path/results.jtl is given, so
the plan is also runnable by hand or from the GUI. Left unset it writes nothing.

error_logging=false: keep every sample. Both labels, POST /tickets and
GET /search, land in the same file and analysis/ separates them by label.</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
        <objProp>
          <name>saveConfig</name>
          <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
            <time>true</time>
            <latency>true</latency>
            <timestamp>true</timestamp>
            <success>true</success>
            <label>true</label>
            <code>true</code>
            <message>true</message>
            <threadName>true</threadName>
            <dataType>true</dataType>
            <encoding>false</encoding>
            <assertions>true</assertions>
            <subresults>true</subresults>
            <responseData>false</responseData>
            <samplerData>false</samplerData>
            <xml>false</xml>
            <fieldNames>true</fieldNames>
            <responseHeaders>false</responseHeaders>
            <requestHeaders>false</requestHeaders>
            <responseDataOnError>false</responseDataOnError>
            <saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>true</saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>
            <assertionsResultsToSave>0</assertionsResultsToSave>
            <bytes>true</bytes>
            <sentBytes>true</sentBytes>
            <url>true</url>
            <threadCounts>true</threadCounts>
            <idleTime>true</idleTime>
            <connectTime>true</connectTime>
          </value>
        </objProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">${__P(jtl_out,)}</stringProp>
      </ResultCollector>
      <hashTree/>
    </hashTree>
  </hashTree>
</jmeterTestPlan>
